<chapter xml:id="ch-baseline-features">
  <title>Baseline Features</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The previous chapters defined the designed item, the state space, and the
      event trajectory. This chapter turns those objects into <term>baseline
      features</term>: measurable summaries of checked answers, state errors,
      movement through the configuration graph, use of checks, timing, and
      cohort-level response patterns.
    </p>

    <p>
      A baseline feature is not, by itself, a claim about student thinking. It is
      a quantity computed from the item design, the key configuration, the
      reconstructed state sequence, and the logged actions. Later chapters use
      these features for diagnostics, statistical models, and validity arguments.
      Here the goal is only to define a stable feature vocabulary.
    </p>

    <p>
      The chapter is organized into six feature families:
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>checked-state features</term>, which compare a submitted
            <xref ref="gt-state" text="custom">state</xref> to the
            <xref ref="gt-key" text="custom">key</xref>;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>state error features</term>, which describe how a checked
            state differs from the key;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>movement features</term>, which summarize ordinary moves
            through the configuration graph;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>segment features</term>, which describe check cadence and
            changes between checked states;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>time features</term>, which use event timestamps;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>cohort features</term>, which aggregate states and
            trajectories across students.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      A preliminary section first fixes what a feature is and at what level of
      analysis it lives.
    </p>

    <p>
      All definitions in this chapter assume an analysis-ready trajectory: events
      have been ordered, <xref ref="gt-positional-labels" text="custom">positional
      labels</xref> have been decoded, states have been reconstructed, and the
      check anchors of <xref ref="sec-check-anchors-and-consistency-checks"/> have
      been compared with the replayed state. Building those tables from raw logs,
      and deciding how to handle reconstruction flags, is the work of
      <xref ref="ch-data-processing"/>. When those flags are raised, the features
      below should be interpreted with caution or excluded according to the
      analysis plan.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-feature-maps">
    <title>Feature Maps</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        A feature is a function that takes one of the framework objects already
        defined and returns a number, vector, table, or label. Some features are
        defined on a single configuration. Others are defined on a trajectory, a
        check segment, or a collection of attempts.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-feature-map-definition">
      <title>Configurations, Trajectories, and Cohorts</title>

      <p>
        Let <m>X\in\mathcal{X}</m> be a valid
        <xref ref="gt-membership-matrix" text="custom">membership matrix</xref>
        and let <m>X^\star</m> be the key membership matrix. A
        <term>state feature</term> is a function of the form
        <md>
          f(X;X^\star).
        </md>
        State features compare one board state to the key. Examples include the
        agreement score, the key distance, and decoy-leak counts.
      </p>

      <p>
        Let <m>\Gamma</m> be an event trajectory and let <m>\phi</m> be the
        feedback policy. A <term>trajectory feature</term> is a function of the
        form
        <md>
          f(\Gamma;X^\star,\phi).
        </md>
        Trajectory features, such as cadence and excess moves, use action marks,
        timestamps, feedback, and the reconstructed state sequence.
      </p>

      <p>
        Finally, if a cohort contains trajectories
        <md>
          \Gamma_1,\Gamma_2,\dots,\Gamma_N,
        </md>
        then a <term>cohort feature</term> aggregates across students, attempts,
        or checked states. Examples include a placement heatmap, a distribution
        of first-check distances, and an exact-correct rate.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-feature-levels">
      <title>Levels of Analysis</title>

      <p>
        The same raw log supports several levels of analysis. Keeping those levels
        separate prevents a feature computed at one level from being interpreted as
        if it belonged to another.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-feature-levels">
        <title>Common feature levels.</title>
        <tabular valign="top" bottom="minor">
          <col width="20%" halign="left"/>
          <col width="35%" halign="left"/>
          <col width="40%" halign="left"/>
          <row bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>Level</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Object</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Typical features</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>state</p></cell>
            <cell><p>one configuration <m>X</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p>agreement, distance, occupancy, decoy leaks</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>checked state</p></cell>
            <cell><p>a state submitted to the grader</p></cell>
            <cell><p>correctness, first-check distance, grader counts</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>move</p></cell>
            <cell><p>a transition <m>X_{k-1}\to X_k</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p>fixing, lateral, breaking, takeback</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>segment</p></cell>
            <cell><p>states between checks or resets</p></cell>
            <cell><p>cadence, post-check gain, responsiveness</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>attempt</p></cell>
            <cell><p>one student's work on one item</p></cell>
            <cell><p>number of checks, excess moves, time to success</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>cohort</p></cell>
            <cell><p>many attempts on one item</p></cell>
            <cell><p>heatmaps, exact-correct rate, transition counts</p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        A state feature describes a board state. A trajectory feature describes a
        path. A cohort feature describes a distribution. These levels are related,
        but they answer different questions. The sections below move from checked
        states, to the structure of their errors, to the paths that produced them,
        to checks and feedback, to time, and finally to the cohort.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-checked-state-features">
    <title>State Features</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Checked-state features compare a configuration to the key. They are the
        closest analogues of ordinary autograded response data: they say whether
        a submitted state was correct and, if not, how far it was from the key.
        The agreement score and key distance are the basic quantities on which the
        later feature families build.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-agreement-vector-baseline">
      <title>Per-Card Agreement</title>

      <p>
        Let <m>X=X(s)</m> be the membership matrix of a configuration <m>s</m>.
        Column <m>j</m> of <m>X</m> is the zone basis vector <m>e_{s(j)}</m>, and
        column <m>j</m> of <m>X^\star</m> is <m>e_{s^\star(j)}</m>. Their dot
        product is <m>1</m> exactly when card <m>j</m> is in its home zone.
        Collecting these dot products gives the <term>per-card agreement
        vector</term>
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-agreement-vector">
          \mathbf{a}(X)
          =
          \operatorname{diag}\bigl(X^{\top}X^\star\bigr)
          \in \{0,1\}^{P},
          \qquad
          a_j
          =
          \begin{cases}
            1, \amp s(j)=s^\star(j),\\
            0, \amp s(j)\neq s^\star(j).
          \end{cases}
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        This vector records which individual cards agree with the key. It does not
        yet compress the response into a single score.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-agreement-score-baseline">
      <title>Agreement Score</title>

      <p>
        Summing the per-card agreements gives a single number. To express that sum
        in matrix form, use the <term>Frobenius inner product</term>
        <md xml:id="an-frobenius-inner-product">
          \langle A,B\rangle_F
          =
          \sum_{i,j}A_{ij}B_{ij}
          =
          \operatorname{tr}\bigl(A^{\top}B\bigr),
        </md>
        with norm <m>\lVert A\rVert_F^2=\langle A,A\rangle_F</m>. Since each
        valid membership matrix has exactly one <m>1</m> in each of its <m>P</m>
        columns,
        <md xml:id="an-membership-frobenius-norm">
          \lVert X\rVert_F^2=P
        </md>
        for every <m>X\in\mathcal{X}</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        The <term>agreement score</term> is the number of cards in their home
        zones:
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-agreement-score">
          \sigma(X)
          =
          \sum_{j=1}^{P} a_j
          =
          \operatorname{tr}\bigl(X^{\top}X^\star\bigr)
          =
          \langle X,X^\star\rangle_F.
        </md>
        It is an integer between <m>0</m> and <m>P</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        A correctly placed card and a correctly trayed
        <xref ref="gt-decoy-card" text="custom">decoy card</xref> both contribute
        <m>1</m> to <m>\sigma(X)</m>. Thus the agreement score can be positive in
        the initial state whenever the key contains decoy cards. The
        partial-credit variants below account for that baseline.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-key-distance-baseline">
      <title>Distance to the Key</title>

      <p>
        The <term>key distance</term> fixes one argument of the state-space
        distance from <xref ref="sec-distance"/> at the key:
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-key-distance">
          d^\star(X)
          =
          d_{\mathcal X}(X,X^\star)
          =
          \frac{1}{2}\|X-X^\star\|_F^2.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Agreement and key distance are two readings of the same comparison.
        Expanding the norm and using
        <m>\lVert X\rVert_F^2=\lVert X^\star\rVert_F^2=P</m> gives
        <md xml:id="an-key-distance-identity">
          d^\star(X)
          =
          \tfrac{1}{2}\bigl(
          \lVert X\rVert_F^2
          +\lVert X^\star\rVert_F^2
          -2\langle X,X^\star\rangle_F
          \bigr)
          =
          P-\sigma(X).
        </md>
        The score counts the cards that agree with the key; the distance counts
        the cards that do not. By the configuration graph of
        <xref ref="sec-configuration-graph"/>, <m>d^\star(X)</m> is also the
        minimum number of ordinary moves needed to reach the key from <m>X</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Exact correctness is the common condition
        <md>
          d^\star(X)=0
          \iff
          \sigma(X)=P
          \iff
          X=X^\star.
        </md>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-grader-counts-baseline">
      <title>Grader Counts</title>

      <p>
        The key distance counts misplaced cards without saying where they sit. Two
        additional counts split that distance by current location. Define
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-wrong-slot-count">
          w(X)
          =
          \#\{j:s(j)\neq 0,\ s(j)\neq s^\star(j)\},
        </md>
        the number of cards currently in a non-tray wrong zone, and
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-left-to-place-count">
          u(X)
          =
          \#\{j:s(j)=0,\ s^\star(j)\neq 0\},
        </md>
        the number of non-decoy cards still left in the tray.
      </p>

      <p>
        Every card is either in its home zone, in a wrong non-tray zone, or
        wrongly in the tray. Hence
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-grader-count-identity">
          \sigma(X)+w(X)+u(X)=P,
        </md>
        and therefore
        <md>
          d^\star(X)=w(X)+u(X).
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        The count <m>w(X)</m> includes non-decoy cards placed in the wrong
        authored slot and decoy cards placed into any authored slot. The count
        <m>u(X)</m> includes only cards that should be placed somewhere but remain
        in the tray. We call <m>w</m> and <m>u</m> <term>grader counts</term>
        because count-level feedback policies can disclose exactly this
        partition: how many cards are wrongly placed, and how many remain to
        place. The state error features of the next section refine this split by
        asking not only where the misplaced cards sit, but where they belong.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-partial-credit-baseline">
      <title>Partial-Credit Variants</title>

      <p>
        Several simple scores are derived from <m>\sigma(X)</m> and
        <m>d^\star(X)</m>. The most basic is the all-or-nothing score
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-all-or-nothing" component="html">
          A(X)
          =
          \mathbb{1}_{[d^\star(X)=0]}
          =
          \begin{cases}
            1, \amp d^\star(X)=0,\\
            0, \amp d^\star(X)\neq 0.
          \end{cases}
        </md>
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-all-or-nothing" component="pdf">
          A(X)
          =
          \mathds{1}_{[d^\star(X)=0]}
          =
          \begin{cases}
            1, \amp d^\star(X)=0,\\
            0, \amp d^\star(X)\neq 0.
          \end{cases}
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        A finer score is the <term>per-card fraction</term>
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-per-card-score">
          p(X)=\frac{\sigma(X)}{P}.
        </md>
        This is the fraction of cards in their home zones, but it rewards the
        initial state for every decoy card whose home zone is the tray.
      </p>

      <p>
        To remove that initial-state baseline, define the
        <term>baseline-corrected progress score</term>
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-corrected-progress">
          g(X)
          =
          \frac{\sigma(X)-\sigma(X_0)}{P-\sigma(X_0)}
          =
          1-\frac{d^\star(X)}{d^\star(X_0)},
        </md>
        whenever <m>d^\star(X_0)\neq 0</m>. This score sets the initial state to
        zero progress and the key to full progress.
      </p>

      <p>
        Unlike a percentage score, <m>g(X)</m> may be negative if the current
        state is farther from the key than the initial state. That sign is useful:
        it records movement away from the starting baseline.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-check-outcome-features">
      <title>Attempt-Level Outcome Features</title>

      <p>
        If an attempt contains checked states
        <md>
          X_{k_1},X_{k_2},\dots,X_{k_q},
        </md>
        then state features can be summarized across check ordinals
        <m>1,\dots,q</m>. When no checked state is correct, the first-success
        ordinal is undefined.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-check-outcome-features">
        <title>Basic outcome features for one attempt.</title>
        <tabular valign="top" bottom="minor">
          <col width="32%" halign="left"/>
          <col width="28%" halign="center"/>
          <col width="40%" halign="left"/>
          <row bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>Feature</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Notation</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Description</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>num_checks</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>q</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p>The number of checked states in the attempt.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>first_check_distance</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>d^\star(X_{k_1})</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p>The distance to the key at the first check.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>final_check_distance</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>d^\star(X_{k_q})</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p>The distance to the key at the last retained check.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>first_check_correct</c></p></cell>
            <cell>
              <p component="html"><m>\mathbb{1}_{[d^\star(X_{k_1})=0]}</m></p>
              <p component="pdf" ><m>\mathds{1}_{[d^\star(X_{k_1})=0]}</m></p>
            </cell>
            <cell><p>Whether the first checked state was correct.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>eventual_correct</c></p></cell>
            <cell>
              <p component="html"><m>\max_i \mathbb{1}_{[d^\star(X_{k_i})=0]}</m></p>
              <p component="pdf" ><m>\max_i \mathds{1}_{[d^\star(X_{k_i})=0]}</m></p>
            </cell>
            <cell><p>Whether any retained checked state was correct.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>correct_check_ordinal</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>\min\{i:d^\star(X_{k_i})=0\}</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p>The first check number on which the response was correct, when it exists.</p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        These outcome features say how many cards were wrong at each check, but
        not what kind of wrong. The next section describes the structure of the
        errors.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-state-error-features">
    <title>State Error Features</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Distance tells how many cards are wrong. State error features describe how
        they are wrong: left in the tray, placed into a wrong authored slot, or
        involved in a decoy error. These are still descriptions of response
        structure, not explanations of why the response occurred.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-zone-confusion-matrix">
      <title>Zone Confusion Matrix</title>

      <p>
        A useful summary of a checked state is the <term>zone confusion
        matrix</term>
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-zone-confusion-matrix">
          M(X)
          =
          XX^{\star\top}.
        </md>
        This is a square matrix of nonnegative integers, with one row and one
        column per zone, indexed from <m>0</m> to <m>R</m>. Its entry
        <m>M_{ih}</m> counts the number of cards currently in zone <m>i</m> whose
        home zone is <m>h</m>:
        <md>
          M_{ih}
          =
          \#\{j:s(j)=i,\ s^\star(j)=h\}.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        The diagonal entries count cards in their home zones, so
        <md>
          \operatorname{tr}(M)=\sigma(X).
        </md>
        Off-diagonal entries identify the source and destination of errors: a card
        counted in <m>M_{ih}</m> currently sits in zone <m>z_i</m> but belongs in
        zone <m>z_h</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Let <m>\mathbf{1}_{R+1}</m> be the all-ones vector indexed by zones.
        Summing across rows gives the current occupancy vector, while summing down
        columns gives the key occupancy vector:
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-confusion-margins">
          M\mathbf{1}_{R+1}=\mathbf{o}(X),
          \qquad
          M^{\top}\mathbf{1}_{R+1}=\mathbf{o}^\star,
          \qquad
          \mathbf{1}^{\top}_{R+1}M\mathbf{1}_{R+1}=P.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        The zone confusion matrix separates several structurally different errors
        that all add one unit to <m>d^\star(X)</m>. The next subsections read those
        error types from particular blocks of <m>M</m>.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-omissions-and-misplacements">
      <title>Omissions and Wrong-Zone Placements</title>

      <aside>
        <title><m>0</m>-indexing</title>
        <p>
          Rows and columns of <m>M</m> are indexed by zones <m>0,1,\dots,R</m>.
          Thus the slot-only block used below has row and column indices
          <m>1,\dots,R</m>.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <p>
        An <term>omission</term> is a card left in the tray even though its home
        zone is an authored slot. The total omission count is the sum of the
        non-tray columns in the tray row:
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-omission-count">
          O(X)
          =
          \sum_{h=1}^{R} M_{0h}.
        </md>
        Comparing with the grader counts, <m>O(X)=u(X)</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        A <term>wrong-zone placement</term> is a non-decoy card placed in the
        wrong authored slot. This is the sum of the off-diagonal entries in the
        slot-by-slot block:
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-wrong-zone-count">
          W_{\operatorname{slot}}(X)
          =
          \sum_{i=1}^{R}
          \sum_{\substack{h=1\\h\neq i}}^{R}
          M_{ih}.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Omissions and wrong-zone placements describe errors involving cards whose
        home zones are authored slots. The remaining distance-contributing errors
        involve decoy cards.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-decoy-leak-features">
      <title>Decoy Leaks</title>

      <p>
        Decoy-card and decoy-slot errors record boundary decisions: whether a card
        should be placed at all, and whether an authored slot should receive any
        card.
      </p>

      <p>
        A <term>decoy-card leak</term> occurs when a card whose home zone is the
        tray is placed into an authored slot. These leaks occupy the tray-home
        column of the confusion matrix:
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-decoy-card-leak">
          L_{\mathcal{P}}(X)
          =
          \sum_{i=1}^{R} M_{i0}.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        A <term>decoy-slot leak</term> occurs when any card is placed into an
        authored slot that is empty in the key. For the decoy-slot set
        <m>\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}</m> defined at the design level, the count is
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-decoy-slot-leak">
          L_{\mathcal{R}}(X)
          =
          \sum_{i\in\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}}}
          \sum_{h=0}^{R} M_{ih}.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        In an item with both decoy cards and decoy slots, one misplaced card can
        contribute to both counts. Placing a decoy card into a decoy slot is both
        a decoy-card leak and a decoy-slot leak. This overlap is intentional: the
        two features describe different aspects of the same placement.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-error-profile">
      <title>Error Decomposition and Error Profile</title>

      <p>
        The first three error counts partition the misplaced cards. A misplaced
        card either sits in the tray while belonging in a slot, sits in a slot
        while belonging in a different slot, or sits in a slot while belonging in
        the tray. Therefore
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-additive-error-decomposition">
          d^\star(X)
          =
          O(X)
          +
          W_{\operatorname{slot}}(X)
          +
          L_{\mathcal{P}}(X).
        </md>
        The decoy-slot count <m>L_{\mathcal{R}}(X)</m> is excluded from this
        decomposition because it overlaps the wrong-zone and decoy-card counts.
      </p>

      <p>
        For a checked state, the basic <term>error profile</term> collects all
        four counts:
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-error-profile">
          \boldsymbol{\varepsilon}(X)
          =
          \bigl(
            O(X),
            W_{\operatorname{slot}}(X),
            L_{\mathcal{P}}(X),
            L_{\mathcal{R}}(X)
          \bigr).
        </md>
        The first three coordinates sum to <m>d^\star(X)</m>. The fourth is an
        overlapping decoy-slot-use indicator, so the profile should be read as a
        decomposition plus one structural flag, not as a four-way partition.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-occupancy-residual">
      <title>Occupancy Residuals</title>

      <p>
        The error profile tracks individual cards. A coarser zone-level summary
        compares only how full each zone is. The current occupancy is
        <m>\mathbf{o}(X)=X\mathbf{1}_P</m>, and the key occupancy is
        <m>\mathbf{o}^\star=X^\star\mathbf{1}_P</m>. Their difference
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-occupancy-residual">
          r(X)
          =
          X\mathbf{1}_P-X^\star\mathbf{1}_P
          =
          M\mathbf{1}_{R+1}-M^{\top}\mathbf{1}_{R+1}
        </md>
        is the <term>occupancy residual</term>. Because both occupancy vectors sum
        to <m>P</m>, the entries of <m>r(X)</m> sum to zero.
      </p>

      <p>
        A positive entry <m>r_i(X)&gt;0</m> means zone <m>z_i</m> currently contains
        more cards than it should. A negative entry <m>r_i(X)&lt;0</m> means the
        zone contains fewer cards than it should.
      </p>

      <p>
        Occupancy residuals ignore which cards caused the imbalance. They are
        coarse features, but they are useful for detecting overfilled and
        underfilled zones and can be computed from zone counts alone.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-trajectory-features">
    <title>Movement Features</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        State features summarize submitted configurations. Movement features use
        the path of states that led to them. They describe movement through the
        configuration graph of <xref ref="sec-configuration-graph"/>; like the
        graph itself, they record what happened on the board, not why it happened.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-distance-curve">
      <title>Distance Curve</title>

      <p>
        Given a reconstructed state sequence
        <md>
          X_0,X_1,\dots,X_n,
        </md>
        the <term>distance curve</term> is
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-distance-curve">
          d_0,d_1,\dots,d_n,
          \qquad
          d_k=d^\star(X_k).
        </md>
        It records the board's distance from the key after each retained state.
      </p>

      <p>
        A monotone decreasing distance curve shows steady movement toward the key.
        Increases and flat steps record regressions and lateral movement in the
        state space. These are movement descriptions, not direct evidence of a
        particular reasoning process.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-move-classification">
      <title>Fixing, Lateral, and Breaking Moves</title>

      <p>
        For an ordinary move from <m>X_{k-1}</m> to <m>X_k</m>, define
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-distance-change">
          \Delta d_k=d^\star(X_k)-d^\star(X_{k-1}).
        </md>
        One ordinary move changes the agreement status of only one card, so
        <md>
          \Delta d_k\in\{-1,0,+1\}.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        We classify the move accordingly:
      </p>

      <p>
        <dl width="medium">
          <li>
            <title>fixing move</title>
            <p>
              <m>\Delta d_k=-1</m>. The move sends a wrong card to its home zone.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>lateral move</title>
            <p>
              <m>\Delta d_k=0</m>. The move changes the board but not its distance
              from the key.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>breaking move</title>
            <p>
              <m>\Delta d_k=+1</m>. The move sends a correct card away from its
              home zone.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        Let <m>F</m>, <m>L</m>, and <m>B</m> be the numbers of fixing, lateral,
        and breaking moves in a reset-free segment of <m>n</m> ordinary moves.
        Then
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-flb-counts">
          F+L+B=n,
        </md>
        and the distance changes telescope to
        <md>
          d^\star(X_{\operatorname{start}})-d^\star(X_{\operatorname{end}})
          =
          F-B.
        </md>
        A reset breaks this telescoping by returning the board to <m>X_0</m> in a
        single action that is not an ordinary move. The counts <m>F</m>, <m>L</m>,
        and <m>B</m> are therefore computed within reset-free segments. Logged
        drops that leave the board unchanged should be flagged as no-op moves and
        excluded from these counts by default.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-excess-moves">
      <title>Excess Moves</title>

      <p>
        The net progress toward the key over a reset-free segment is <m>F-B</m>.
        The <term>excess-move count</term> records the part of the movement that
        does not contribute to that net progress:
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-excess-moves">
          E_{\operatorname{move}}
          =
          n-(F-B)
          =
          2B+L.
        </md>
        A lateral move contributes one excess move. A breaking move contributes
        two: one move to create the regression and one later fixing move to undo
        it. Equivalently,
        <m>E_{\operatorname{move}}
        =n-\bigl(d^\star(X_{\operatorname{start}})-d^\star(X_{\operatorname{end}})\bigr)</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        A stricter comparison measures the walk against its own endpoints rather
        than against the key. The <term>path excess</term> is
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-path-excess">
          E_{\operatorname{path}}
          =
          n-d_{\mathcal{X}}(X_{\operatorname{start}},X_{\operatorname{end}}),
        </md>
        the number of moves beyond the shortest path between the observed start
        and end states. Since a walk is at least as long as the graph distance
        between its endpoints, <m>E_{\operatorname{path}}\geq 0</m>. The triangle
        inequality gives
        <md>
          0
          \leq
          E_{\operatorname{path}}
          \leq
          E_{\operatorname{move}}.
        </md>
        The gap between them is motion that shortened the distance to neither the
        key nor the segment's own destination.
      </p>

      <p>
        For a reset-free attempt that starts at the initial state and ends at the
        key, <m>d_{\mathcal{X}}(X_0,X^\star)=d^\star(X_0)</m>, so the two measures
        coincide:
        <md>
          E_{\operatorname{move}}
          =
          E_{\operatorname{path}}
          =
          n-d^\star(X_0).
        </md>
        For partial trajectories, check segments, and exploratory paths, they may
        differ.
      </p>

      <p>
        Excess moves are baseline inefficiency measures. They should be read
        alongside item design and feedback policy, since informative feedback can
        change the movement pattern a student has reason to follow.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-takeback-and-reset-features">
      <title>Takebacks and Resets</title>

      <p>
        A <term>takeback</term> is an ordinary move whose destination is the tray.
        Its count is
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-takeback-count">
          T
          =
          \#\{k:\text{ordinary move }a_k\text{ sends a card to }z_0\}.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        A takeback can be fixing, lateral, or breaking, depending on
        <m>\Delta d_k</m>. Returning a decoy card to the tray is fixing.
        Returning a correctly placed non-decoy card to the tray is breaking.
        Returning a wrong card from one wrong location to the tray may be lateral
        or fixing, depending on the card's home zone.
      </p>

      <p>
        A <term>reset</term> is not an ordinary move. It returns the board to
        <m>X_0</m> in one logged action. The reset count is
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-reset-count">
          R_{\operatorname{reset}}
          =
          \#\{k:a_k\text{ is a reset}\}.
        </md>
        Resets should be counted separately from ordinary moves and takebacks, and
        they delimit the segments within which <m>F</m>, <m>L</m>, <m>B</m>, and
        the excess-move features are computed.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-repeat-and-cycle-features">
      <title>Repeated States and Cycles</title>

      <p>
        Since a trajectory is a walk through the configuration graph, it may
        revisit states. The number of distinct states visited is
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-distinct-states">
          V(\Gamma)
          =
          \#\{X_k:0\leq k\leq n\}.
        </md>
        The number of repeated state visits is
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-repeated-states">
          R_{\operatorname{state}}(\Gamma)
          =
          (n+1)-V(\Gamma).
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Repeated states show that the board returned to a previously seen
        configuration. This can occur through deliberate revision, feedback use,
        accidental movement, or exploration. The feature records the repetition;
        it does not identify the reason.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-check-and-feedback-features">
    <title>Segment Features</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Movement features treat ordinary moves as a single path. Checks add
        structure: they mark states, divide the trace into segments, and return
        feedback determined by <m>\phi</m>. The features in this section describe
        when checks occur and how checked states change afterward. They should be
        interpreted in light of what the feedback policy actually disclosed.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-check-cadence">
      <title>Check Cadence</title>

      <p>
        Let
        <md>
          k_1&lt;k_2&lt;\cdots&lt;k_q
        </md>
        be the indices of the checked states in a trajectory, as in the check
        index set of the trajectory model. Write <m>n_i</m> for the number of
        ordinary moves between check <m>i</m> and check <m>i+1</m>, and write
        <m>n_0</m> for the number of ordinary moves before the first check. The
        <term>check cadence</term> is the sequence of move counts
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-check-cadence">
          \operatorname{cadence}_i=n_i,
          \qquad
          i=0,1,\dots,q-1.
        </md>
        The leading term <m>n_0</m>, the <term>first-check cadence</term>, is
        defined even when a student checks only once.
      </p>

      <p>
        Cadence is a trace feature under a feedback policy. Short cadence may
        indicate frequent use of feedback, but its interpretation depends on what
        the check returns and whether checks are limited or penalized.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-post-check-gain">
      <title>Post-Check Gain</title>

      <p>
        If two consecutive checked states are <m>X_{k_i}</m> and
        <m>X_{k_{i+1}}</m>, the <term>post-check gain</term> from check <m>i</m>
        to check <m>i+1</m> is
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-post-check-gain">
          G_i
          =
          d^\star(X_{k_i})-d^\star(X_{k_{i+1}}).
        </md>
        A positive value means the next checked state is closer to the key. A zero
        value means no net checked-state progress. A negative value means the next
        checked state is farther from the key.
      </p>

      <p>
        This feature ignores what happened between the two checks. A student may
        make many moves, including regressions and repairs, while ending at the
        same distance. The movement features above record that intermediate work;
        in particular, segment-level excess moves measure motion that the gain
        does not see.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-responsiveness-baseline">
      <title>Responsiveness to Feedback</title>

      <p>
        Some feedback policies identify specific cards that are wrong at a check.
        Suppose check <m>i</m> marks a set <m>\mathcal{H}_i\subseteq\mathcal{P}</m>
        of highlighted cards, and let <m>\mathcal{M}_i\subseteq\mathcal{P}</m> be
        the set of cards moved before the next check. Define the
        <term>responsiveness</term>
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-responsiveness">
          \rho_i
          =
          \frac{|\mathcal{H}_i\cap \mathcal{M}_i|}{|\mathcal{H}_i|},
        </md>
        when <m>|\mathcal{H}_i|&gt;0</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        The value <m>\rho_i</m> measures how much of the highlighted set was acted
        on before the next check. It is defined only when the feedback policy
        identifies cards. If the policy returns only Correct or Incorrect, then
        <m>\mathcal{H}_i</m> is not observed and responsiveness is not defined.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-time-features">
    <title>Time Features</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The features so far use event order but not the clock. Each logged action
        also carries a timestamp, and time features use differences between
        timestamps to summarize pauses, pacing, and time to key events.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-action-gaps">
      <title>Action Gaps</title>

      <p>
        The elementary timing quantity is the gap
        <m>\delta_k=t_k-t_{k-1}</m> before event <m>k</m>, introduced in the
        trajectory model. Gaps can be computed for all consecutive logged actions
        or restricted to particular action types. Restricting to consecutive moves
        gives move gaps; restricting to consecutive checks gives check gaps.
      </p>

      <p>
        Large gaps may reflect thinking time, interruption, distraction, page
        idling, or technical issues. Time features should therefore be cleaned and
        interpreted cautiously. The next chapter defines rules for trimming or
        flagging extreme idle periods.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-basic-time-features">
      <title>Basic Timing Summaries</title>

      <p>
        Let <m>m_1</m> be the index of the first ordinary move, when one exists,
        and let <m>k_1</m> be the index of the first check, when one exists. The
        basic timing summaries are:
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-basic-time-features">
        <title>Basic time features for one attempt.</title>
        <tabular valign="top" bottom="minor">
          <col width="32%" halign="left"/>
          <col width="28%" halign="center"/>
          <col width="40%" halign="left"/>
          <row bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>Feature</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Notation</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Description</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>total_time</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>t_n-t_0</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Elapsed time from first to last retained event in the attempt.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>time_to_first_move</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>t_{m_1}-t_0</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Time from opening or initial state to the first ordinary move.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>time_to_first_check</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>t_{k_1}-t_0</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Time from opening or initial state to the first check.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>median_move_gap</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><mdash/></p></cell>
            <cell><p>The median time between consecutive move actions.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>max_action_gap</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>\max_k \delta_k</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p>The largest observed gap between consecutive logged actions.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>active_time</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><mdash/></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Total time after excluding gaps larger than a chosen idle threshold.</p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-cohort-features">
    <title>Cohort Features</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The previous feature families describe one student's work. Cohort features
        aggregate many students' states or trajectories on the same item. They are
        useful for item diagnostics because they show which cards, zones, states,
        and transitions are common.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-placement-heatmap">
      <title>Placement Heatmap</title>

      <p>
        Suppose <m>N</m> students produce checked states
        <md>
          X^{(1)},X^{(2)},\dots,X^{(N)}
        </md>
        for the same item at the same analysis point. The <term>placement
        heatmap</term> is the average membership matrix
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-placement-heatmap">
          \bar{X}
          =
          \frac{1}{N}\sum_{\nu=1}^{N}X^{(\nu)}.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        The entry <m>\bar{X}_{ij}</m> is the empirical fraction of students who
        placed card <m>j</m> in zone <m>z_i</m> at the chosen analysis point. Each
        column of <m>\bar{X}</m> sums to one, but its entries are proportions
        rather than zeros and ones.
      </p>

      <p>
        Heatmaps can be computed at different analysis points: first check, final
        check, first correct check, incorrect checks only, or a fixed point in a
        reconstructed trajectory. The aggregation rule should be reported because
        different choices answer different questions.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-card-placement-entropy">
      <title>Card Placement Entropy</title>

      <p>
        The placement distribution for card <m>j</m> is the <m>j</m>-th column of
        <m>\bar{X}</m>. Its entropy is
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-card-entropy">
          H_j
          =
          -\sum_{i=0}^{R}
          \bar{X}_{ij}\log(\bar{X}_{ij}),
        </md>
        using the natural logarithm and the convention <m>0\log(0)=0</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Low entropy means most students placed the card in the same zone. High
        entropy means placements were spread across several zones. Entropy measures
        dispersion; it does not say whether the common placement is correct.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-cohort-correctness-rates">
      <title>Correctness Rates and Distance Distributions</title>

      <p>
        The all-or-nothing score of the checked-state section aggregates to the
        <term>exact-correct rate</term> at a chosen analysis point:
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-exact-correct-rate" component="html">
          \bar{A}
          =
          \frac{1}{N}\sum_{\nu=1}^{N}
          A\bigl(X^{(\nu)}\bigr)
          =
          \frac{1}{N}\sum_{\nu=1}^{N}
          \mathbb{1}_{[d^\star(X^{(\nu)})=0]}.
        </md>
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-exact-correct-rate" component="pdf">
          \bar{A}
          =
          \frac{1}{N}\sum_{\nu=1}^{N}
          A\bigl(X^{(\nu)}\bigr)
          =
          \frac{1}{N}\sum_{\nu=1}^{N}
          \mathds{1}_{[d^\star(X^{(\nu)})=0]}.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        The mean distance is
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-mean-distance">
          \bar{d}^\star
          =
          \frac{1}{N}\sum_{\nu=1}^{N}
          d^\star(X^{(\nu)}).
        </md>
        The full distance distribution should also be retained, since two cohorts
        can have the same mean distance but different error patterns.
      </p>

      <p>
        These aggregates differ in what they require. The mean agreement score,
        and hence the mean distance, is linear in the states and can be recovered
        from the heatmap alone:
        <m>P-\bar{d}^\star=\langle\bar{X},X^\star\rangle_F</m>. The exact-correct
        rate cannot. Exact correctness depends on each student's whole
        configuration, not only on the marginal placement frequencies recorded in
        <m>\bar{X}</m>.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-transition-counts">
      <title>Transition Counts</title>

      <p>
        A cohort of trajectories also gives a transition table on the
        configuration graph. For configurations <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m>, define
        <md xml:id="an-baseline-transition-count">
          C_{XY}
          =
          \#\{\text{observed ordinary moves }X\to Y\}.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        These counts identify common moves, common wrong states, and common paths
        through the item. They are descriptive network summaries, not stochastic
        models. Later analyses may normalize them into transition probabilities or
        compare transition networks across items, courses, or semesters.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-running-example-features">
    <title>The Running Example</title>

    <p>
      We now compute baseline features for the running shapes question, using the
      attempt assembled in the previous chapter. The key is
      <md>
        s^\star=(1,1,2,0),
      </md>
      so the initial state <m>s_0=(0,0,0,0)</m> has one card already correct: the
      circle, whose home zone is the tray. Therefore
      <md>
        \sigma(X_0)=1,
        \qquad
        d^\star(X_0)=3.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Considering only ordinary moves, the student follows the trajectory
      <md>
        (0,0,0,0)
        \to
        (1,0,0,0)
        \to
        (1,1,0,0)
        \to
        (1,1,2,0)
        \to
        (1,1,2,3)
        \to
        (1,1,2,0),
      </md>
      with checks after the fourth and fifth moves. The distance curve is
      <md>
        3\to2\to1\to0\to1\to0.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      The first checked state is <m>s=(1,1,2,3)</m>: the two triangles and the
      square are correct, while the circle sits in the Pentagon slot. Its
      checked-state features are
      <md>
        \sigma(X)=3,
        \qquad
        d^\star(X)=1,
        \qquad
        w(X)=1,
        \qquad
        u(X)=0,
      </md>
      and its baseline-corrected progress is
      <m>g(X)=1-\tfrac{1}{3}=\tfrac{2}{3}</m>. The single misplaced card is placed
      rather than left in the tray, so the grader counts assign it to <m>w</m>
      rather than <m>u</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The zone confusion matrix of the first checked state, with rows and columns
      indexed by zones <m>0</m> through <m>3</m>, is
      <md>
        M(X)
        =
        XX^{\star\top}
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
          0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
          0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
          1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </md>
      The diagonal confirms <m>\operatorname{tr}(M)=3=\sigma(X)</m>. The single
      off-diagonal entry <m>M_{30}=1</m> is a card currently in zone <m>z_3</m>
      whose home zone is <m>z_0</m>. Reading off the error counts gives
      <md>
        \boldsymbol{\varepsilon}(X)
        =
        \bigl(O,W_{\operatorname{slot}},L_{\mathcal{P}},L_{\mathcal{R}}\bigr)
        =
        (0,0,1,1).
      </md>
      The first three coordinates sum to <m>d^\star(X)=1</m>: the misplaced card
      is a decoy-card leak. Because it landed in the empty Pentagon slot, it is
      also a decoy-slot leak. The occupancy residual is
      <m>r(X)=(-1,0,0,1)^\top</m>: the tray is one card short and the Pentagon slot
      is one card over.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-running-example-baseline-features">
      <title>Baseline features for the running example trajectory.</title>
      <tabular valign="top" bottom="minor">
        <col width="34%" halign="left"/>
        <col width="26%" halign="center"/>
        <col width="40%" halign="left"/>
        <row bottom="medium">
          <cell><p>Feature</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Value</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Comment</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><m>\sigma(X_0)</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>1</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p>The decoy card is already correctly in the tray.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><m>d^\star(X_0)</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>3</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Three non-decoy cards still need to be placed.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>first checked state</p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>(1,1,2,3)</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p>The circle has been placed into the Pentagon slot.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>first-check distance</p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>1</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Only the circle is wrong.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>grader counts <m>(w,u)</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>(1,0)</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p>One card wrongly placed; none left to place.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>error profile <m>\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>(0,0,1,1)</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p>No omissions or wrong-slot placements; one decoy-card leak that is also a decoy-slot leak.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>first-check cadence <m>n_0</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>4</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Four moves before the first check.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>post-check gain <m>G_1</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>1</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p>The second checked state is one move closer to the key.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>eventual correct</p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>1</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p>The second checked state is the key.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><m>F,L,B</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>4,0,1</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Four fixing moves, no lateral moves, and one breaking move.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>takebacks <m>T</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>1</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p>The circle is returned to the tray after the incorrect check.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>excess moves <m>E_{\operatorname{move}}</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>2</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p>The breaking move away from the key must be undone.</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      Since this attempt is reset-free, starts at the initial state, and ends at
      the key, the two excess measures coincide:
      <m>E_{\operatorname{move}}=E_{\operatorname{path}}=n-d^\star(X_0)=5-3=2</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The example shows why baseline features are useful. The first checked state
      is only one move from the key, but the error is structurally specific: a
      decoy card has been placed into a decoy slot. The distance, error profile,
      and movement features describe different parts of the response, while the
      takeback after the incorrect check connects the feedback event to a repair.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-feature-dictionary-summary">
    <title>Minimal Feature Dictionary</title>

    <p>
      The following table summarizes a minimal baseline feature set for export.
      Additional features may be added for particular research questions, but this
      set is enough to describe basic outcomes, state errors, movement, checks,
      timing, and cohort summaries.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-minimal-baseline-feature-dictionary">
      <title>Minimal baseline feature dictionary.</title>
      <tabular valign="top" bottom="minor">
        <col width="22%" halign="left"/>
        <col width="30%" halign="left"/>
        <col width="48%" halign="left"/>
        <row bottom="medium">
          <cell><p>Family</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Features</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Purpose</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>checked state</p></cell>
          <cell><p><c>sigma</c>, <c>distance</c>, <c>per_card_score</c>, <c>corrected_progress</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Compare a submitted configuration to the key.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>outcome</p></cell>
          <cell><p><c>first_check_correct</c>, <c>eventual_correct</c>, <c>correct_check_ordinal</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Summarize correctness across checks.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>state errors</p></cell>
          <cell><p><c>omissions</c>, <c>wrong_slot</c>, <c>decoy_card_leak</c>, <c>decoy_slot_leak</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Describe the type of mismatch with the key.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>trajectory</p></cell>
          <cell><p><c>num_moves</c>, <c>fixing</c>, <c>lateral</c>, <c>breaking</c>, <c>excess_moves</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Summarize movement through the configuration graph.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>checks</p></cell>
          <cell><p><c>num_checks</c>, <c>cadence</c>, <c>post_check_gain</c>, <c>responsiveness</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Describe use of the check button and response to feedback.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>time</p></cell>
          <cell><p><c>total_time</c>, <c>active_time</c>, <c>time_to_first_move</c>, <c>time_to_first_check</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Summarize pacing and elapsed time.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>cohort</p></cell>
          <cell><p><c>placement_heatmap</c>, <c>card_entropy</c>, <c>exact_correct_rate</c>, <c>transition_counts</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Aggregate responses across students or attempts.</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      Two remarks close the chapter. First, all definitions above assume
      analysis-ready trajectories. The next chapter describes how those tables are
      built from raw logs and how reconstruction problems are flagged; the
      visualization chapter then turns the features defined here into item-level
      diagnostic displays.
    </p>

    <p>
      Second, these baseline features keep the original card and slot labels
      fixed: a card is correct only in its exact home zone.
      <xref ref="ch-group-features"/> relaxes that requirement by
      considering label-free agreement, grouping structure, merge and split
      features, and partition-based comparison metrics.
    </p>
  </section>
</chapter>
